const PATTERN_ACADEMY_16 = {
  "lcs": {
    title: "Pattern 68 -- LCS / Sequence DP",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "We're entering a NEW major DP family. So far, DP has mostly been items+target/capacity. Now it's sequence A + sequence B + positions. The central question: how do two sequences relate to each other as we move through them? LCS, Edit Distance, Shortest Common Supersequence, Delete Operation, Uncrossed Lines, Distinct Subsequences, Interleaving String, sequence alignment -- many are variations of this one fundamental idea.",
        conclusion: "Knapsack: items + capacity. Sequence DP: two sequences + positions." },
      { type: "text", heading: "1. First: Subsequence",
        body: "A = [1,2,3,4]. A subsequence can be [1,3,4] because order is preserved -- but [3,1] is NOT a subsequence. It doesn't need to be contiguous." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "2. LCS -- Longest Common Subsequence", dark: true,
        body: "A = \"abcde\", B = \"ace\". Common subsequences include \"a\", \"ac\", \"ae\", \"ace\". Longest = \"ace\". Answer = 3." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "3. Why LCS Needs DP", tone: "red",
        body: "A naive approach tries take/skip A[i] and take/skip B[j], creating exponential possibilities. But many recursive branches arrive at the SAME state (i, j) -- meaning 'what is the answer using suffixes/prefixes starting at these positions?' We cache that state." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "4. Mental Model", dark: true,
        body: "A: a b c d e\n     ^\n     i\n\nB: a c e\n     ^\n     j\n\nAt any point (i, j), ask: what is the best common subsequence using these two positions onward? That gives dp[i][j]." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "5-7. State Definition & Base Case", dark: true,
        body: "Prefix version (recommended -- cleaner base cases): dp[i][j] = LCS length between the first i characters of A and the first j characters of B.\n\nIf one string is empty, LCS(\"\", anything) = 0. So: dp[0][j] = 0; dp[i][0] = 0 -- the entire first row and first column are zero." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "8. The Critical Recurrence -- Match", tone: "gold",
        body: "If A[i-1] == B[j-1], that character can be part of the LCS. dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1. Intuitive: previous LCS + matching character." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "9. What If Characters Don't Match?", tone: "red",
        body: "If A[i-1] != B[j-1], they cannot both be the next character of the common subsequence -- one must be excluded. Two possibilities: skip A[i-1] or skip B[j-1]. dp[i][j] = max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]). This is the most important LCS recurrence." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "10. Master Formula", dark: true,
        body: "if A[i-1] == B[j-1]:\n    dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1\nelse:\n    dp[i][j] = max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1])\n\nMemorize the REASON, not just the formula." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "11. Java", dark: true,
        body: "public int longestCommonSubsequence(String text1, String text2) {\n    int m = text1.length();\n    int n = text2.length();\n    int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];\n\n    for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {\n        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {\n            if (text1.charAt(i - 1) == text2.charAt(j - 1)) {\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;\n            } else {\n                dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    return dp[m][n];\n}\n\nComplexity: Time = O(mn), Space = O(mn)." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "12-13. Why Diagonal on Match, Up/Left on Mismatch", dark: true,
        body: "A=\"abc\", B=\"adc\". At A[2]=c, B[2]=c -- they match. Using c means moving BOTH sequences forward: (i-1,j-1) becomes (i,j). Hence diagonal.\n\nIf A[i]=x, B[j]=y and they can't match: either discard x (go up) or discard y (go left), and take the better one.\n\nMATCH -> diagonal.\nNO MATCH -> up / left." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "14. Visualize the DP Grid", dark: true,
        body: "A=\"abcde\", B=\"ace\":\n\n        \"\"  a  c  e\n     \"\"  0  0  0  0\n     a   0\n     b   0\n     c   0\n     d   0\n     e   0\n\nEach cell asks: what is the LCS of these two prefixes? The answer builds toward dp[5][3] = 3." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "15. The Deepest Recognition Rule", tone: "gold",
        body: "When you see two sequences and need to compare their relative ordering, think Sequence DP. If there are two independent positions i and j, strongly consider dp[i][j] -- one of the most useful DP recognition signals." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "16-18. LCS vs Longest Common Substring", items: [
        { label: "Skip characters?", body: "LCS: Yes. Common Substring: No." },
        { label: "Must be contiguous?", body: "LCS: No. Common Substring: Yes." },
        { label: "Match", body: "Both: diagonal + 1." },
        { label: "Mismatch", body: "LCS: max(up, left). Common Substring: reset to 0 (contiguity is broken)." }
      ], conclusion: "A=\"abcde\", B=\"abfde\": longest common substring is \"ab\" or \"de\" (length 2), while LCS(\"abcde\",\"abfde\") is longer since it can skip characters. Different DP transitions entirely." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "19-20. LCS vs LIS vs Edit Distance", items: [
        { label: "LCS", body: "Two sequences. Compares A[i-1] and B[j-1]." },
        { label: "LIS", body: "One sequence + an increasing condition -- a completely different core recurrence/optimization. Don't confuse them." },
        { label: "Edit Distance", body: "Asks 'minimum operations to transform one string into another' rather than 'longest preserved sequence,' but uses the same dp[i][j] comparing A[i-1]/B[j-1] -- same broader Sequence DP family." }
      ]},
      { type: "callout", heading: "21-23. LCS Reconstruction", tone: "gold",
        body: "Sometimes the interviewer wants the actual LCS, not just its length. Walk backward from i=m, j=n: if A[i-1]==B[j-1], that character belongs to the LCS (append it, i--, j--). Otherwise, move toward whichever of dp[i-1][j] or dp[i][j-1] is larger (i-- or j--). Reverse the result at the end.\n\nWhy it works: the DP table contains the decision history implicitly -- match means diagonal, mismatch means it came from up or left. 'DP table = compressed decision tree' generalizes to many DP problems." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "22. Java Reconstruction", dark: true,
        body: "public String getLCS(String a, String b) {\n    int m = a.length(), n = b.length();\n    int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];\n\n    for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++)\n        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++)\n            dp[i][j] = (a.charAt(i-1) == b.charAt(j-1))\n                ? dp[i-1][j-1] + 1\n                : Math.max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]);\n\n    StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();\n    int i = m, j = n;\n    while (i > 0 && j > 0) {\n        if (a.charAt(i-1) == b.charAt(j-1)) {\n            result.append(a.charAt(i-1));\n            i--; j--;\n        } else if (dp[i-1][j] >= dp[i][j-1]) {\n            i--;\n        } else {\n            j--;\n        }\n    }\n    return result.reverse().toString();\n}" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "24. Reduction -- Shortest Common Supersequence", dark: true,
        body: "a=\"abac\", b=\"cab\" -- find the shortest string containing both as subsequences. This LOOKS like a new problem, but it reduces to LCS: SCS length = m + n - L, where L = LCS(a,b). Common characters don't need to be duplicated." },
      { type: "text", heading: "25. LCS and Delete Operations",
        body: "Minimum deletions to make two strings equal: if L = LCS(a,b), keep the LCS and delete everything else. Deletions = (m-L) + (n-L), so minimum deletions = m + n - 2*LCS. Another reduction to LCS." },
      { type: "text", heading: "26. Uncrossed Lines",
        body: "'Connect equal values between two arrays without crossing lines' looks like a geometry problem, but the answer is LCS -- because non-crossing connections preserve order. An excellent example of interview pattern recognition." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "27. Recognition Upgrade",
        intro: "Ask: does this problem secretly ask for the longest ordered common selection between two sequences? Potential disguises:",
        items: ["common sequence", "matching elements", "maximum non-crossing matches", "minimum deletions to equalize", "shortest common supersequence", "sequence alignment"] },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "28-30. State Variants & Deriving the Recurrence", dark: true,
        body: "Prefix: dp[i][j] = LCS of first i and first j (recommended -- cleaner base cases). Suffix: dp[i][j] = LCS from i,j onward. Both work.\n\nDon't memorize 'match -> diagonal+1, else -> max(up,left).' Derive it: (1) look at A[i-1] and B[j-1]; (2) are they equal? Yes -> use both -> diagonal. No -> can't use both together -> remove one -> max(skip A, skip B)." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "31. Why Not Take Both When They Don't Match?", tone: "red",
        body: "A[i]='a', B[j]='b'. A common subsequence cannot contain 'a' and 'b' at the SAME matching position -- so at least one must be excluded. That's why we branch into max(up, left) instead of trying to combine them." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "32-33. Complexity & Space Optimization", dark: true,
        body: "m x n states, O(1) work each: Time = O(mn), Space = O(mn).\n\nEach row only depends on the previous row plus the current row's left value, so O(mn) can shrink to O(n) with two rows (or one, with care):\n\nint[] prev = new int[n+1];\nint[] curr = new int[n+1];\nfor (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {\n    for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {\n        curr[j] = (a.charAt(i-1)==b.charAt(j-1))\n            ? prev[j-1] + 1\n            : Math.max(prev[j], curr[j-1]);\n    }\n    int[] tmp = prev; prev = curr; curr = tmp;\n    Arrays.fill(curr, 0);\n}\n\nDon't optimize prematurely in interviews unless asked." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "34. Important Interview Advice", tone: "gold",
        body: "First give O(mn) time / O(mn) space -- easiest to explain and to reconstruct the actual sequence from. THEN say: 'If we only need the length, we can reduce space to O(min(m,n)).' That's much stronger than jumping straight to an optimized implementation." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "35. The Bigger Family", dark: true,
        body: "SEQUENCE DP\n |\n +-- LCS\n +-- Longest Common Substring\n +-- Edit Distance\n +-- Shortest Common Supersequence\n +-- Distinct Subsequences\n +-- Interleaving String\n +-- Delete Distance\n +-- Sequence Alignment\n\nThese are NOT independent core patterns. The core idea: positions in one/multiple sequences + state representing the relationship between them." },
      { type: "practice", heading: "36. How to Recognize Sequence DP",
        intro: "Ask in order:",
        items: [
          "Q1. Are there two sequences? Yes -> suspect dp[i][j].",
          "Q2. Does the problem compare their elements? Yes -> strong LCS/edit-distance family signal.",
          "Q3. Does order matter? Yes -> sequence DP.",
          "Q4. Do I make decisions based on current characters (match? skip? insert? delete?) -> likely sequence DP."
        ]},
      { type: "diagram", heading: "37. Compare Our DP Families", dark: true,
        body: "DP\n |\n +-- Linear DP:    dp[i]\n +-- Grid DP:      dp[i][j] = position\n +-- Knapsack:     item + capacity\n +-- Partition:    prefix + number of groups\n +-- Sequence DP:  sequence A position + sequence B position\n\nThe state is determined by what information the FUTURE needs. That's the real DP skill." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "38. The Most Important LCS Muscle Memory", dark: true,
        body: "        B ->\n     +-----------\nA |  |\n  v  |   dp[i][j]\n     |\n\nsame?\n  |\n +-+-+\nYES NO\n |   |\n v   +-- skip A\ndiag +-- skip B\n+1" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "39. LCS Pattern Card", dark: true,
        body: "CORE PATTERN: Sequence DP\n\nTrigger: two ordered sequences need comparison.\nState: dp[i][j]\nMeaning: best relationship between prefixes of length i and j.\nMatch: dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1\nMismatch: dp[i][j] = max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1])\nTime: O(mn)\nSpace: O(mn)" },
      { type: "callout", heading: "40. What You Should NOT Memorize", tone: "red",
        body: "Don't memorize 'LeetCode 1143 = this code.' Instead: two sequences -> dp[i][j] -> compare current elements -> match -> diagonal, mismatch -> skip one. Then derive the code. That's how you stop depending on memorization." },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 7 progress",
        items: [{ label: "68. LCS / Sequence DP", done: true }, { label: "69. Longest Common Substring", done: false },
          { label: "70. Edit Distance", done: false }, { label: "71. Shortest Common Superseq", done: false },
          { label: "72. Distinct Subsequences", done: false }, { label: "73. Interleaving String", done: false },
          { label: "74. LIS", done: false }, { label: "75. Palindrome DP", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Longest Common Substring",
        points: ["Worth doing right after LCS because the two problems look almost identical on the surface", "The one recurrence change that separates them: LCS mismatch -> max(up, left); Substring mismatch -> reset to 0", "Understanding WHY that single change happens will make your Sequence DP recognition much stronger"] }
    ]
  },
  "longest-common-substring": {
    title: "Pattern 69 -- Longest Common Substring",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "Longest Common Substring is NOT a brand-new core pattern -- it's a variation of Sequence DP / LCS. The difference is one constraint: the substring must be contiguous. That single requirement completely changes the mismatch transition.",
        conclusion: "One word -- contiguous -- flips the entire recurrence." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "1. LCS vs Longest Common Substring", items: [
        { label: "LCS", body: "A=\"abcde\", B=\"abfde\". Can skip characters -> \"abde\", length 4." },
        { label: "Longest Common Substring", body: "Same strings, must be contiguous. Common substrings include \"ab\" and \"de\" -> longest length 2." }
      ], conclusion: "LCS -> subsequence -> can skip. Common Substring -> contiguous -> cannot skip." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "2. Recognition Trigger",
        intro: "Think Longest Common Substring when you see:",
        items: ["longest common contiguous sequence", "longest matching block", "longest common segment", "common substring", "matching consecutive characters/elements"],
        note: "The word CONTIGUOUS is the biggest trigger." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "3. Mental Model", dark: true,
        body: "A: a b c d e\n         ^\n\nB: a b f d e\n         ^\n\ndp[i][j] means: length of the longest common substring ENDING AT A[i-1] and B[j-1]. That last phrase is critical.\n\nFor LCS: dp[i][j] = best anywhere in the prefixes.\nFor substring: dp[i][j] = best ending exactly here." },
      { type: "text", heading: "4. Why Does \"Ending Here\" Matter?",
        body: "Because substring must be contiguous. A=\"abc\", B=\"axc\". At A[2]=c, B[2]=c they match. But the characters immediately before (A[1]=b, B[1]=x) don't match. So the common substring ending at c has length 1 -- we cannot jump back to some older match." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "5. The Recurrence", tone: "red",
        body: "If A[i-1] == B[j-1]: dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1 -- exactly like LCS. But if A[i-1] != B[j-1]: dp[i][j] = 0. That's the crucial difference." },
      { type: "text", heading: "6. Why Mismatch = 0?",
        body: "The substring has been broken. A: a b c d, B: a b X d. At d they match, but the characters immediately before (c vs X) don't. So we cannot say \"abd\" is a common substring -- the current contiguous run starts over as just \"d\", giving dp[i][j] = 0 + 1 = 1." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "7. The Master Formula", dark: true,
        body: "if A[i-1] == B[j-1]:\n    dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1\nelse:\n    dp[i][j] = 0\n\nThen maintain: answer = max(answer, dp[i][j])." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "8. Java Solution", dark: true,
        body: "public int longestCommonSubstring(String a, String b) {\n    int m = a.length();\n    int n = b.length();\n    int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];\n    int answer = 0;\n\n    for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {\n        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {\n            if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)) {\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;\n                answer = Math.max(answer, dp[i][j]);\n            } else {\n                dp[i][j] = 0;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    return answer;\n}\n\nComplexity: Time = O(mn), Space = O(mn)." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "9. The Biggest Difference From LCS", items: [
        { label: "Can skip?", body: "LCS: Yes. Common Substring: No." },
        { label: "Contiguous?", body: "LCS: No. Common Substring: Yes." },
        { label: "Match", body: "Both: diagonal + 1." },
        { label: "Mismatch", body: "LCS: max(up,left). Common Substring: 0." },
        { label: "Answer", body: "LCS: dp[m][n]. Common Substring: maximum anywhere in the table." }
      ], conclusion: "One of the highest-value comparison tables in Sequence DP." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "10-11. Why LCS Uses max(up,left) but Substring Can't", tone: "red",
        body: "LCS doesn't require continuity: A=\"abc\", B=\"axc\", at c==c we can use it even though the previous characters don't match, because LCS can carry forward an earlier common subsequence -- so mismatch preserves the best via max(up,left).\n\nFor substring, that same carried-forward sequence is no longer CONTIGUOUS with the current position -- using max(up,left) would incorrectly stitch together a non-contiguous match. So substring must reset to 0 on mismatch. This is the fundamental reason for the difference." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "12. The State Definition Is Everything", tone: "gold",
        body: "LCS: dp[i][j] = best LCS within prefixes. Substring: dp[i][j] = best common substring ENDING at i,j. That tiny difference in wording creates the entire recurrence difference.",
        conclusion: "A good state definition almost automatically gives you the transition." },
      { type: "text", heading: "13-14. Example & Why the Answer Isn't Always dp[m][n]",
        body: "A=\"abcxyz\", B=\"abxyz\": longest common substring is \"xyz\", length 3 -- the DP region shows 1,2,3 along the matching diagonal, and the answer is the maximum value, not necessarily dp[m][n].\n\nA=\"xyzabc\", B=\"abc\": the match happens to end at dp[m][n]=3. But A=\"abcxyz\", B=\"abc\": the answer is still 3, but the maximum could occur earlier in the table depending on positions. So: answer = max over ALL dp[i][j], never assume dp[m][n]." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "15-16. Reconstruction -- Return the Actual Substring", dark: true,
        body: "If asked to return the substring itself, not just its length: track maxLength and endIndex. Whenever dp[i][j] > maxLength, save maxLength=dp[i][j] and endIndex=i. Then return a.substring(endIndex - maxLength, endIndex).\n\npublic String longestCommonSubstring(String a, String b) {\n    int m = a.length(), n = b.length();\n    int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];\n    int maxLength = 0, endIndex = 0;\n\n    for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {\n        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {\n            if (a.charAt(i-1) == b.charAt(j-1)) {\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1;\n                if (dp[i][j] > maxLength) {\n                    maxLength = dp[i][j];\n                    endIndex = i;\n                }\n            } else {\n                dp[i][j] = 0;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    return a.substring(endIndex - maxLength, endIndex);\n}" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "17. 1D Space Optimization", dark: true,
        body: "Only the previous row is needed, but dp[i-1][j-1] must still be available -- so process j backward with one array, or use two rows for clarity:\n\nint[] prev = new int[n + 1];\nint[] curr = new int[n + 1];\nint answer = 0;\n\nfor (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {\n    for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {\n        if (a.charAt(i-1) == b.charAt(j-1)) {\n            curr[j] = prev[j-1] + 1;\n            answer = Math.max(answer, curr[j]);\n        } else {\n            curr[j] = 0;\n        }\n    }\n    int[] temp = prev; prev = curr; curr = temp;\n    Arrays.fill(curr, 0);\n}\n\nreturn answer;\n\nSpace: O(n)." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "18. Recognition: Substring vs Subsequence vs Subset", dark: true,
        body: "CONTIGUOUS -> Substring / Subarray\nORDER PRESERVED, CAN SKIP -> Subsequence\nORDER IRRELEVANT -> Subset\n\nOne of the most important interview triggers -- these three words point to three completely different DP structures." },
      { type: "text", heading: "19. String Version vs Array Version",
        body: "Don't restrict this pattern to strings. A=[1,2,3,4], B=[2,3,5,6] -> longest common contiguous sequence is [2,3]. Same DP. The true pattern is Longest Common CONTIGUOUS Sequence -- strings are just one representation." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "20. Connection to Computer Science",
        intro: "Useful for real-world applications (not needed for LeetCode, but helps explain why the pattern exists):",
        items: ["DNA sequence comparison", "plagiarism detection", "log matching", "binary sequence comparison", "document comparison", "pattern matching", "bioinformatics"] },
      { type: "text", heading: "21. Why Does This Pattern Exist?",
        body: "Sometimes we don't care about all common elements -- we care about the longest continuous region shared by two sequences. A: ...ABCDEFG..., B: ...XYZDEFG... -> the common region DEFG is a contiguous block. That's fundamentally different from A: ABXCDYE, B: AQBCZDE, where a common subsequence can skip around freely." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "22. Pattern Classification", dark: true,
        body: "CORE: Sequence DP\nVARIATION: Longest Common Substring\nRelated variation: LCS\nKey difference: contiguity\n\nSequence DP\n  -> Common Sequence Problems\n       -> LCS\n       -> Longest Common Substring\n\nDon't file this as an independent algorithm -- file it under Sequence DP." },
      { type: "variants", heading: "23-24. Common Mistakes", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Using max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]) for substring", note: "That's the LCS recurrence. For substring on mismatch, dp[i][j] = 0. Remember: LCS mismatch -> preserve old answer. Substring mismatch -> continuity broken -> restart." },
        { id: "2", title: "Returning dp[m][n]", note: "Wrong in general for longest common substring -- the best substring can end anywhere in the table. You need max(dp[i][j]) over the whole table." }
      ]},
      { type: "callout", heading: "25. Interview Explanation", tone: "gold",
        body: "'Because the matching characters must be contiguous, I'll use a two-dimensional sequence DP. dp[i][j] represents the length of the common substring ending at a[i-1] and b[j-1]. If the characters match, I extend the previous diagonal by one. If they don't, the contiguous sequence is broken, so the state becomes zero. I'll track the maximum value across the table.'",
        conclusion: "A very clean, derivation-based explanation." },
      { type: "practice", heading: "26. Recognition Drill",
        intro: "Match the phrasing to the family:",
        items: [
          "A. 'Longest sequence in both strings, characters don't have to be adjacent' -> LCS.",
          "B. 'Longest contiguous matching block' -> Longest Common Substring.",
          "C. 'Longest increasing subsequence' -> LIS.",
          "D. 'Contiguous subarray with maximum sum' -> Kadane / Prefix-style techniques.",
          "E. 'Select elements to reach target sum' -> Subset Sum."
        ],
        note: "Notice how the exact words tell you the family." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "27. Sequence DP Family So Far", dark: true,
        body: "Sequence DP\n  |\n  +-- LCS\n  |     +-- Shortest Common Supersequence\n  |     +-- Delete Distance\n  |     +-- Uncrossed Lines\n  |\n  +-- Common Substring\n\nAnd soon: Edit Distance, Distinct Subsequences, Interleaving." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "28. Master Comparison", dark: true,
        body: "                TWO SEQUENCES\n                     |\n          +----------+----------+\n          v                     v\n       SUBSEQUENCE          SUBSTRING\n          |                     |\n       can skip             contiguous\n          |                     |\n         LCS               Common Substring\n          |                     |\n mismatch:                mismatch:\n max(up,left)                  0\n\nThis should become muscle memory." },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 7 progress",
        items: [{ label: "68. LCS / Sequence DP", done: true }, { label: "69. Longest Common Substring", done: true },
          { label: "70. Edit Distance", done: false }, { label: "71. Shortest Common Superseq", done: false },
          { label: "72. Distinct Subsequences", done: false }, { label: "73. Interleaving String", done: false },
          { label: "74. LIS", done: false }, { label: "75. Palindrome DP", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Edit Distance",
        points: ["A major Sequence DP pattern: two strings, compare current characters, match -> diagonal, mismatch -> three operations (insert, delete, replace)", "The important skill isn't memorizing the three transitions -- it's understanding WHY each operation corresponds to a different neighboring DP state", "Building on the exact dp[i][j] comparison muscle memory from LCS and Longest Common Substring"] }
    ]
  },
  "edit-distance": {
    title: "Pattern 70 -- Edit Distance",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "Unlike LCS and Longest Common Substring, this is a genuine major interview pattern in its own right, also called Levenshtein Distance. Classic problem: what is the minimum number of operations (insert, delete, replace) needed to transform string A into string B?",
        conclusion: "With LCS we ask what two sequences share. With Edit Distance we ask how much work it takes to turn one into the other." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "1. Core Pattern Example", dark: true,
        body: "A = \"horse\", B = \"ros\".\n\nhorse -> delete h -> orse -> replace e with s -> ors -> delete r -> os\n\nThe optimal sequence needs 3 operations." },
      { type: "text", heading: "2. Why Does This Pattern Exist?",
        body: "At every position we compare A[i] and B[j]. If they match: no operation needed. If they don't: insert, delete, or replace. So dp[i][j] is again the natural state -- but now the objective is minimum transformation cost, not shared structure." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "3. Recognition Triggers",
        intro: "Think Edit Distance when you see:",
        items: ["minimum operations to transform one string into another", "convert string A into string B", "insert/delete/replace", "edit operations", "character transformations", "spelling correction", "string similarity", "minimum edits", "Levenshtein distance"],
        note: "Strongest trigger: minimum insertions, deletions, and replacements to transform one string into another." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "4-5. State & Base Cases", dark: true,
        body: "dp[i][j] = minimum number of operations required to transform the first i characters of A into the first j characters of B.\n\nIf A is empty and B=\"abc\": need 3 insertions, so dp[0][j] = j.\nIf B is empty and A=\"abc\": need 3 deletions, so dp[i][0] = i." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "6-7. Characters Match vs Don't Match", tone: "gold",
        body: "If A[i-1] == B[j-1]: no operation is necessary, so dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] -- simply move both pointers.\n\nIf A[i-1] != B[j-1]: three choices are available -- delete, insert, replace. This is the heart of Edit Distance." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "8-10. Delete, Insert, Replace -- One At A Time", dark: true,
        body: "Delete: A=\"abc\", B=\"ac\" -- delete b. We consumed one character from A but nothing from B, so delete = dp[i-1][j].\n\nInsert: A=\"ac\", B=\"abc\" -- insert b. We consume one character from B, A hasn't moved, so insert = dp[i][j-1].\n\nReplace: A=\"cat\", B=\"cut\" -- replace a with u. We consume one character from each string, so replace = dp[i-1][j-1]." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "11-12. The Master Recurrence & Why +1", tone: "red",
        body: "If A[i-1] == B[j-1]: dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1].\nOtherwise: dp[i][j] = 1 + min(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1], dp[i-1][j-1]).\n\nWhy +1? Because we just performed one operation -- 1 operation plus the optimal solution for the remaining strings.",
        conclusion: "This is the entire algorithm." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "13. Java Solution", dark: true,
        body: "public int minDistance(String word1, String word2) {\n    int m = word1.length();\n    int n = word2.length();\n    int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];\n\n    for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++) dp[i][0] = i;\n    for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) dp[0][j] = j;\n\n    for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {\n        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {\n            if (word1.charAt(i - 1) == word2.charAt(j - 1)) {\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1];\n            } else {\n                dp[i][j] = 1 + Math.min(dp[i - 1][j],\n                    Math.min(dp[i][j - 1], dp[i - 1][j - 1]));\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    return dp[m][n];\n}\n\nComplexity: Time = O(mn), Space = O(mn)." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "14. The Most Important Mental Picture", dark: true,
        body: "       B ->\n     0  1  2  3\nA  0\nv  1\n   2\n   3\n\nEach cell dp[i][j] can come from: diagonal (up-left), up, or left.\n\nUP -> delete from A\nLEFT -> insert into A (consume from B)\nDIAGONAL -> replace" },
      { type: "callout", heading: "15-17. Why Insert is LEFT, Delete is UP, Replace is DIAGONAL", tone: "gold",
        body: "Insert: A=\"cat\", B=\"cats\" -- inserting s consumes B's s but A hasn't moved -> dp[i][j-1] -> LEFT.\n\nDelete: consumes A[i] but B stays put -> dp[i-1][j] -> UP.\n\nReplace: consumes one character from each -> dp[i-1][j-1] -> DIAGONAL.",
        conclusion: "Memory aid: UP consumes A, LEFT consumes B, DIAGONAL consumes both." },
      { type: "text", heading: "18. Worked Example",
        body: "A=\"ab\", B=\"ac\". At b vs c: mismatch. Options: delete b, insert c, or replace b with c. Replacement is cheapest at cost 1, so dp[2][2] = 1." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "19. Edit Distance vs LCS", items: [
        { label: "LCS", body: "Objective: maximize common subsequence. Match -> diagonal + 1. Mismatch -> max(up, left)." },
        { label: "Edit Distance", body: "Objective: minimize operations. Match -> diagonal (no +1). Mismatch -> 1 + min(up, left, diagonal)." }
      ], conclusion: "Same state structure, dp[i][j], but a completely different optimization objective." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "20. Edit Distance vs Common Substring", items: [
        { label: "Common Substring", body: "Mismatch -> 0, because continuity breaks and we can't modify anything." },
        { label: "Edit Distance", body: "Mismatch -> 1 + min(...), because we ARE allowed to modify the strings." }
      ], conclusion: "The meaning of the problem determines the transition -- not the shape of the grid." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "21. The Bigger Sequence DP Family", dark: true,
        body: "Sequence DP\n  |\n  +-- LCS\n  |     +-- SCS\n  |     +-- Delete Distance\n  |     +-- Uncrossed Lines\n  |\n  +-- Common Substring\n  |\n  +-- Edit Distance\n\nThe same dp[i][j] state appears repeatedly across this whole family." },
      { type: "text", heading: "22-24. Three LCS-Based Reductions",
        body: "Delete-only distance (delete from A or B, no insert/replace): if L = LCS(A,B), keep the LCS and delete everything else -> (m-L) + (n-L) = m + n - 2L.\n\nShortest Common Supersequence: SCS length = m + n - L, because the shared LCS characters don't need to be duplicated.\n\nEdit Distance with only insert/delete (no replace): deletions = m-L, insertions = n-L, total = m + n - 2L -- identical to the delete-only case above." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "25. Edit Distance With Weighted Operations", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose insert=1, delete=2, replace=5. Same DP -- only the transition costs change: delete -> dp[i-1][j]+2, insert -> dp[i][j-1]+1, replace -> dp[i-1][j-1]+5.",
        conclusion: "The DP structure stays the same; the operation cost changes. A very useful generalization." },
      { type: "text", heading: "26. Edit Distance With No Replace",
        body: "If replacement isn't allowed: dp[i][j] = 1 + min(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]) when characters differ. Same structure, one fewer option." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "27. Edit Distance With Transposition", tone: "red",
        body: "Some variants allow 'ab -> ba' as a single operation. Then the state needs additional information about previous characters -- when the allowed operation depends on history, the state must capture that history. That's the universal DP state-design principle we've been building all along." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "28-29. Space Optimization", dark: true,
        body: "Each row depends on the previous row plus the current row -- so O(mn) can shrink to O(n) if only the distance is needed. The tricky part is preserving dp[i-1][j-1] (the diagonal) before it gets overwritten:\n\nint[] dp = new int[n + 1];\nfor (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) dp[j] = j;\n\nfor (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {\n    int diagonal = dp[0];\n    dp[0] = i;\n    for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {\n        int temp = dp[j];\n        if (a.charAt(i-1) == b.charAt(j-1)) {\n            dp[j] = diagonal;\n        } else {\n            dp[j] = 1 + Math.min(temp, Math.min(dp[j-1], diagonal));\n        }\n        diagonal = temp;\n    }\n}\n\nIf you need the actual sequence of edits, keep the full table instead. Don't lead with this optimized version in an interview -- start with the clear 2D version." },
      { type: "practice", heading: "30. Recognition Drill",
        intro: "Match the phrasing to the family:",
        items: [
          "'Convert kitten to sitting using minimum insert/delete/replace' -> Edit Distance.",
          "'Find the longest sequence shared by two strings' -> LCS.",
          "'Find the longest contiguous sequence shared by two strings' -> Longest Common Substring.",
          "'Find minimum deletions to make two strings equal' -> LCS reduction.",
          "'Find shortest string containing both strings as subsequences' -> LCS reduction (SCS)."
        ]},
      { type: "comparison", heading: "31. The Most Important State Design Lesson", items: [
        { label: "LCS", body: "dp[i][j] = best answer in prefixes." },
        { label: "Common Substring", body: "dp[i][j] = best answer ending here." },
        { label: "Edit Distance", body: "dp[i][j] = minimum operations for prefixes." }
      ], conclusion: "Same i x j dimensions, three different meanings. Don't choose a recurrence first -- define exactly what dp[i][j] MEANS first. That's the biggest difference between memorizing DP and being able to solve new DP problems." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "32. The Interview Derivation", tone: "gold",
        body: "If you forget the formula: are the current characters equal? Yes -> no operation -> diagonal. No -> what operations are allowed? delete consumes A, insert consumes B, replace consumes both -- so up, left, diagonal, take the minimum (plus 1 for the operation performed). You can reconstruct the entire algorithm from this reasoning alone." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "33. Pattern Classification", dark: true,
        body: "CORE PATTERN: Sequence DP / Edit Distance\n\nState: dp[i][j]\nDimension: two sequence positions\nObjective: minimum transformation cost\n\nTransitions:\nmatch -> diagonal (no cost)\nmismatch:\n  delete  -> up\n  insert  -> left\n  replace -> diagonal (+1)" },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 7 progress",
        items: [{ label: "68. LCS / Sequence DP", done: true }, { label: "69. Longest Common Substring", done: true },
          { label: "70. Edit Distance", done: true }, { label: "71. Shortest Common Superseq", done: false },
          { label: "72. Distinct Subsequences", done: false }, { label: "73. Interleaving String", done: false },
          { label: "74. LIS", done: false }, { label: "75. Palindrome DP", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Shortest Common Supersequence",
        points: ["Reinforcing reduction thinking: SCS -> LCS -> shared characters -> avoid duplicating them", "SCS length = m + n - LCS(A,B), building directly on the reductions covered in this lesson", "Then moving to Distinct Subsequences, where the objective shifts from 'best length' to counting the number of ways"] }
    ]
  },
  "shortest-common-supersequence": {
    title: "Pattern 71 -- Shortest Common Supersequence",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "Classification first: this is NOT a completely new core DP pattern -- it is a variation/reduction of LCS. SCS -> LCS -> find the characters both strings can share -> merge everything else around that shared sequence.",
        conclusion: "A very important interview skill: reduce a new-looking problem to a pattern you already know." },
      { type: "text", heading: "1. What Is a Supersequence?",
        body: "A string S is a supersequence of another string if the original string appears as a SUBSEQUENCE of S. A=\"ab\" -> supersequences include \"ab\", \"abc\", \"xab\", \"axb\", because \"ab\" can be obtained by deleting characters from any of them." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "2. Shortest Common Supersequence", dark: true,
        body: "A=\"abac\", B=\"cab\". Find the shortest string containing both as subsequences. One answer: \"cabac\" (length 5). Check: \"abac\" and \"cab\" can both be extracted from \"cabac\" by deleting characters." },
      { type: "text", heading: "3. Why Isn't This Just Concatenation?",
        body: "You could always do A+B: \"abac\"+\"cab\" = \"abaccab\" -- definitely a common supersequence, but not necessarily shortest. We want to share characters whenever possible. That's where LCS comes in." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "4-5. The Key Insight & The Formula", tone: "red",
        body: "A=\"abac\", B=\"cab\". Their LCS is \"ab\". Those characters already appear in both strings -- instead of putting them into the final result twice, we share them.\n\nLet m=A.length(), n=B.length(), L=LCS(A,B). Then: SCS length = m + n - L. Here: 4 + 3 - 2 = 5.",
        conclusion: "One of the most useful reductions in this family." },
      { type: "text", heading: "6. Why the Formula Works",
        body: "A: ABCD, B: BCE. Common subsequence: BC. Without sharing: A contributes 4, B contributes 3, total 7 -- but BC already exists in A, so we don't add those characters again. Overlap = 2, so 7 - 2 = 5." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "7. Recognition Trigger",
        intro: "When you see: find the shortest string that contains both strings as subsequences. Immediately think:",
        items: ["SCS -> LCS", "length only? -> m + n - LCS", "no separate SCS DP needed for length alone"] },
      { type: "text", heading: "8-9. What If They Ask for the Actual String?",
        body: "Now it's more interesting -- we reuse the LCS DP table to reconstruct the SCS itself. This is where understanding the DP table matters far more than knowing the formula.\n\nMental model: A=\"abac\", B=\"cab\". Start from the end (i=m, j=n) and ask: how should these two characters appear in the final supersequence?" },
      { type: "callout", heading: "10-12. Match, Mismatch, and Letting the LCS Table Decide", tone: "gold",
        body: "If A[i-1] == B[j-1] (e.g. both 'c'): we only need one copy -- append it once, move diagonally (i--, j--). Exactly the same diagonal movement as LCS.\n\nIf they differ, we need to include both characters somehow, but which comes first? Use the LCS table: if dp[i-1][j] > dp[i][j-1], the optimal common subsequence continues through A[i-1], so append A[i-1] and move i--. Otherwise append B[j-1] and move j--." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "13. Reconstruction Algorithm", dark: true,
        body: "Start at i=m, j=n:\n\nCase 1 -- one string exhausted: if i==0, append the remaining B. If j==0, append the remaining A.\nCase 2 -- characters equal: append once (answer += A[i-1]), i--, j--.\nCase 3 -- characters differ: if dp[i-1][j] > dp[i][j-1], append A[i-1] and i--. Otherwise append B[j-1] and j--.\n\nFinally reverse the result." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "14. Java Implementation", dark: true,
        body: "public String shortestCommonSupersequence(String str1, String str2) {\n    int m = str1.length(), n = str2.length();\n    int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];\n\n    for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {\n        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {\n            if (str1.charAt(i-1) == str2.charAt(j-1)) {\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1;\n            } else {\n                dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();\n    int i = m, j = n;\n    while (i > 0 && j > 0) {\n        if (str1.charAt(i-1) == str2.charAt(j-1)) {\n            result.append(str1.charAt(i-1)); i--; j--;\n        } else if (dp[i-1][j] > dp[i][j-1]) {\n            result.append(str1.charAt(i-1)); i--;\n        } else {\n            result.append(str2.charAt(j-1)); j--;\n        }\n    }\n    while (i > 0) { result.append(str1.charAt(i-1)); i--; }\n    while (j > 0) { result.append(str2.charAt(j-1)); j--; }\n\n    return result.reverse().toString();\n}" },
      { type: "callout", heading: "15. Why Does the Reconstruction Work?", tone: "gold",
        body: "The LCS table tells us which characters should be shared. The SCS construction does the opposite: add every character from both strings, but add LCS characters only once. Think: A characters + B characters - shared LCS characters -> SCS length = m + n - LCS." },
      { type: "text", heading: "16-17. Worked Examples",
        body: "A=\"ab\", B=\"ac\". LCS=\"a\". SCS length = 2+2-1=3. Possible SCS: \"abc\" -- both \"ab\" and \"ac\" are subsequences.\n\nA=\"geek\", B=\"eke\". LCS=\"ek\", length 2. SCS length = 4+3-2=5. One possible answer: \"geeke\"." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "18. SCS vs LCS", items: [
        { label: "LCS", body: "Keep as many shared characters as possible -- maximize overlap." },
        { label: "SCS", body: "Build a sequence containing both while minimizing total length -- minimize the merged sequence." }
      ], conclusion: "The bridge: maximum overlap = LCS, therefore minimum merged size = m + n - LCS." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "19. This Is a General Algorithmic Idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "The deeper pattern: minimization through maximum overlap. If two things have sizes m and n, and some part can be shared with overlap L, then merged size = m + n - overlap. In SCS, overlap = LCS. This style of reasoning appears in many algorithmic problems beyond strings." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "20. SCS as a Reduction", dark: true,
        body: "CORE: Sequence DP\nVARIATION: LCS\nREDUCTION: SCS -> LCS\n\nProblem says 'shortest sequence containing both'\n  -> think 'what can I share?'\n       -> longest shared subsequence\n            -> LCS\n\nThis is exactly why SCS should never be memorized as an independent pattern." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "21. Supersequence vs Subsequence", items: [
        { label: "Subsequence", body: "Take something OUT of a string: A=abcde -> subsequence=ace (remove)." },
        { label: "Supersequence", body: "Build something that CONTAINS the string: A=ace -> supersequence=abcde (add)." }
      ]},
      { type: "callout", heading: "22-24. Common Mistake & the Two Formulas", tone: "red",
        body: "Common mistake: using m + n - 2*LCS for SCS. That formula is actually for minimum deletions from BOTH strings to make them equal (A needs m-L deletions, B needs n-L deletions, total m+n-2L).\n\nFor SCS, only subtract L ONCE, because the shared characters are represented once instead of twice: SCS length = m + n - L." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "24. Compare the Two Formulas", items: [
        { label: "Minimum deletions to make equal", body: "m + n - 2L -- delete from both sides, subtract twice." },
        { label: "Shortest common supersequence", body: "m + n - L -- share once, subtract once." }
      ], conclusion: "MAKE EQUAL -> delete both sides -> subtract twice. MERGE -> share once -> subtract once." },
      { type: "text", heading: "25. Another Reduction: Uncrossed Lines",
        body: "'Connect equal numbers between two arrays without crossing lines' looks like a graph/geometry problem. But non-crossing + preserve order means LCS. Weird-looking problem -> identify structure -> LCS. This is exactly the recognition skill we're developing." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "26. Sequence DP Map", dark: true,
        body: "Sequence DP\n  |\n  +-- LCS\n  |     +-- SCS\n  |     +-- Delete Distance\n  |\n  +-- Substring\n  |\n  +-- Edit Distance\n\nLCS is becoming a central reusable pattern, not just one LeetCode question." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "27-28. Interview Answers -- Length Only vs Actual String", tone: "gold",
        body: "Length only: 'I'll use LCS. Any common subsequence can be shared once in the supersequence, so if the strings have lengths m and n and their LCS has length L, the SCS length is m + n - L. I'll compute LCS in O(mn).'\n\nActual string: 'I'll first compute the LCS table. Then walk backward: if the current characters match, include the character once and move diagonally. Otherwise follow the larger LCS value, append that character, and move in that direction. Once one string is exhausted, append the remaining characters from the other.'" },
      { type: "practice", heading: "29. Recognition Drill",
        intro: "Match the phrasing to the family:",
        items: [
          "'Longest sequence common to both' -> LCS.",
          "'Longest contiguous sequence common to both' -> Longest Common Substring.",
          "'Shortest string containing both as subsequences' -> SCS -> LCS.",
          "'Minimum deletions to make both strings equal' -> LCS.",
          "'Minimum insertions + deletions to transform A to B' -> LCS.",
          "'Minimum insert/delete/replace' -> Edit Distance."
        ],
        note: "You're now seeing the family, not individual questions." },
      { type: "practice", heading: "30. The Real Muscle Memory",
        intro: "When a string problem appears, don't ask 'have I seen this exact LeetCode problem?' Ask:",
        items: [
          "1. One string or two?",
          "2. Substring or subsequence?",
          "3. Are we maximizing, minimizing, or counting?",
          "4. Are we transforming or comparing?",
          "5. Can the problem reduce to LCS?"
        ],
        note: "That will identify a huge number of problems." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "31. Pattern Card", dark: true,
        body: "VARIATION / REDUCTION: SCS\n\nTrigger: shortest sequence containing both strings as subsequences.\nCore: LCS\nFormula: SCS length = m + n - LCS\nActual string: build using the LCS table.\n\nMatch: take character once -> diagonal\nMismatch: follow larger LCS direction -> take one character" },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 7 progress",
        items: [{ label: "68. LCS / Sequence DP", done: true }, { label: "69. Longest Common Substring", done: true },
          { label: "70. Edit Distance", done: true }, { label: "71. Shortest Common Superseq", done: true },
          { label: "72. Distinct Subsequences", done: false }, { label: "73. Interleaving String", done: false },
          { label: "74. LIS", done: false }, { label: "75. Palindrome DP", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Distinct Subsequences",
        points: ["An important shift: LCS/Substring maximize length, Edit Distance minimizes operations, SCS minimizes length -- now Distinct Subsequences COUNTS the number of ways", "The structure looks similar to LCS, but the transition changes from max(...) to count + count", "Starting to see DP as a counting framework, not just an optimization framework"] }
    ]
  },
  "distinct-subsequences": {
    title: "Pattern 72 -- Distinct Subsequences",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "This is an important shift in objective. So far: LCS maximizes length, Edit Distance minimizes operations, SCS minimizes length. Distinct Subsequences COUNTS the number of ways. It's a Sequence DP variation, but the counting technique itself is extremely reusable." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "1. The Problem", dark: true,
        body: "s = \"rabbbit\", t = \"rabbit\". How many distinct subsequences of s equal t? Answer: 3.\n\nWhy? We need to remove one of the three b's: r a [b b b] i t -- three different choices produce \"rabbit\"." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "2. Recognition Trigger",
        intro: "Think Distinct Subsequences when you see:",
        items: ["number of ways", "count subsequences", "form target from source", "delete characters", "preserve relative order", "source string to target string", "count ways to transform using deletion"],
        note: "Strongest trigger: how many ways can I form target from source while preserving order? -> think Sequence DP + Counting." },
      { type: "text", heading: "3-4. The State & An Example",
        body: "dp[i][j] = number of ways to form the first j characters of t using the first i characters of s. This definition is extremely important: dp[i][j] means first i of s can form first j of t.\n\nExample: s=\"babgbag\", t=\"bag\" -- the DP counts every valid way to pick b, a, g in order from the source while preserving relative positions." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "5-6. Two Base Cases", tone: "gold",
        body: "Base case 1: number of ways to create an empty target (t=\"\") is exactly 1 -- choose nothing. So dp[i][0] = 1 for every i. Same counting principle as Coin Change: ways to create zero = 1.\n\nBase case 2: number of ways to create a non-empty target from an empty source (s=\"\", t=\"abc\") is impossible. So dp[0][j] = 0 for j > 0. Summary: dp[0][0]=1, dp[i][0]=1, dp[0][j]=0." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "7-9. The Critical Decision -- Match vs Mismatch", tone: "red",
        body: "At s[i-1] vs t[j-1]:\n\nMismatch: this source character cannot be used to match the current target character. Only one choice -- skip it. dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j].\n\nMatch: two choices exist. Use this character (both pointers move): dp[i-1][j-1]. Or don't use it (skip it): dp[i-1][j]. Since these are mutually exclusive paths: dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + dp[i-1][j]." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "10. Master Formula vs LCS", items: [
        { label: "LCS", body: "Match -> dp[i-1][j-1] + 1. Mismatch -> max(up, left)." },
        { label: "Distinct Subsequences", body: "Match -> dp[i-1][j-1] + dp[i-1][j]. Mismatch -> dp[i-1][j]." }
      ], conclusion: "Because we're counting choices, not maximizing a score." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "11. Java Solution", dark: true,
        body: "public int numDistinct(String s, String t) {\n    int m = s.length(), n = t.length();\n    long[][] dp = new long[m + 1][n + 1];\n\n    // One way to create empty target: choose nothing.\n    for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++) dp[i][0] = 1;\n\n    for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {\n        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {\n            if (s.charAt(i - 1) == t.charAt(j - 1)) {\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + dp[i - 1][j];\n            } else {\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j];\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    return (int) dp[m][n];\n}" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "12. The Mental Model", dark: true,
        body: "At every source character: should I use it?\n  YES -> consume source and target\n  NO  -> skip source\n\nWhen characters don't match: NO choice, must skip.\n\nMATCH -> use OR skip -> ADD\nMISMATCH -> skip only" },
      { type: "callout", heading: "13-14. Why ADD Instead of MAX", tone: "gold",
        body: "If two choices produce different decision paths, total ways = A + B -- that's why dp[i-1][j-1] + dp[i-1][j]. We're counting mutually exclusive choices.\n\nLCS wants the best answer -> max(...). Distinct Subsequences wants the total number of valid possibilities -> sum(...). Ask: am I choosing the best option, or counting all valid options? That determines the operator: optimization -> min/max, counting -> +." },
      { type: "text", heading: "15-16. Worked Example: aaa -> aa",
        body: "Source \"aaa\", target \"aa\". Choose any two of the three a's: positions (1,2), (1,3), (2,3) -- answer = 3 = C(3,2). The DP discovers this naturally.\n\nAt the final a: dp[3][2] = dp[2][1] (use it) + dp[2][2] (skip it) = 2 + 1 = 3." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "17-18. Why There's No dp[i][j-1]", tone: "red",
        body: "In LCS mismatch we use max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]) because two sequences compete to form a common subsequence. Here, the source provides characters and the target is the fixed pattern we must form -- we only ever scan the source. If a source character doesn't match, we skip THAT source character: dp[i-1][j]. We never have dp[i][j-1], because that would mean skipping a required target character without consuming a source character -- not a valid subsequence construction." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "19-20. Recursive Version & Mental Model", dark: true,
        body: "ways(i, j) = number of ways to form t[j...] using s[i...].\n\nIf i == s.length(): return 0, unless j == t.length(), in which case return 1.\n\nMismatch: ways(i,j) = ways(i+1,j).\nMatch: ways(i,j) = ways(i+1,j+1) + ways(i+1,j). Memoize it -- same DP.\n\nsource[i] == target[j]?\n  USE  -> i+1,j+1\n  SKIP -> i+1,j\nsource[i] != target[j]?\n  SKIP -> i+1,j" },
      { type: "text", heading: "21. Why Is It Called Distinct?",
        body: "s=\"aaa\", t=\"aa\" -- the resulting string is always \"aa\", but the WAYS of choosing positions differ: (1,2), (1,3), (2,3). So 'distinct' refers to distinct subsequence selections/ways, not necessarily different resulting text values." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "22-23. Recognition & A Non-DP Cousin",
        intro: "'Given source S and target T, count the number of ways to delete characters from S to obtain T' -> source + target + preserve order + count -> Distinct Subsequences DP.",
        items: ["Related but different: 'how many words are subsequences of s' (s=abcde, words=[a,bb,acd,ace]) is usually solved with index lists + two pointers, not DP"],
        note: "Not every problem containing the word subsequence is Sequence DP." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "24. Distinct Subsequences vs LCS", items: [
        { label: "Goal", body: "LCS: maximum length. Distinct Subsequences: count ways." },
        { label: "Match / Mismatch", body: "LCS: diagonal+1 / max(up,left). Distinct Subsequences: diagonal+skip / skip source." },
        { label: "Operator", body: "LCS: max. Distinct Subsequences: +." },
        { label: "Target fixed?", body: "LCS: No. Distinct Subsequences: Yes." }
      ]},
      { type: "comparison", heading: "25. Distinct Subsequences vs Subset Count", items: [
        { label: "Subset Count", body: "items + target sum, order generally doesn't matter -> dp[sum]." },
        { label: "Distinct Subsequences", body: "source sequence + target sequence, order MUST be preserved -> dp[i][j]." }
      ], conclusion: "Subset -> selection. Subsequence -> ordered selection." },
      { type: "text", heading: "26-27. Connection to Knapsack & Counting DP",
        body: "0/1 Knapsack: at each item, take or skip. Distinct Subsequences: at each source character, use or skip. Structurally: Distinct Subsequences = ordered 0/1 take/skip counting + a target matching constraint.\n\nCoin Change II uses dp[amount] +=. Distinct Subsequences uses dp[i][j] +=. Same fundamental principle: when multiple independent valid paths reach a state, add their counts. One of the most important counting-DP patterns." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "28. Overflow", tone: "red",
        body: "This problem can produce very large counts -- with long repeated-character strings the number of ways can become enormous. Don't blindly use int inside the DP; prefer long if constraints allow, or apply dp[i][j] %= MOD if a modulus is specified." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "29-30. Space Optimization & Why Backward", dark: true,
        body: "Each row depends only on the previous row, so O(mn) can shrink to O(n). Subtlety: we need both dp[i-1][j-1] and dp[i-1][j], so with one array, process j BACKWARD.\n\nIf dp[j] currently represents the previous row, we need the previous dp[j-1] for the diagonal. Moving j from n down to 1 means dp[j-1] hasn't been overwritten yet -- exactly the same principle as 0/1 Knapsack's backward capacity loop." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "31-32. 1D Version & Why Not Forward", dark: true,
        body: "public long numDistinct(String s, String t) {\n    int n = t.length();\n    long[] dp = new long[n + 1];\n    dp[0] = 1;\n\n    for (int i = 1; i <= s.length(); i++) {\n        for (int j = n; j >= 1; j--) {\n            if (s.charAt(i - 1) == t.charAt(j - 1)) {\n                dp[j] += dp[j - 1];\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    return dp[n];\n}\n\nGoing forward (j=1 to n) would let dp[j-1] already include the CURRENT source character, allowing it to be reused multiple times -- invalid. Backward prevents that." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "33-34. A Powerful Connection & What If Repetition Were Allowed", tone: "gold",
        body: "0/1 Knapsack: for each item, for capacity descending. Distinct Subsequences: for each source character, for target position descending. Both mean: current item/character can be used at most once.\n\nIf a source character could be reused multiple times, the iteration would flip to forward -- the same general rule you've now seen in both Knapsack and Sequence counting: each input element once -> backward, unlimited reuse -> forward." },
      { type: "variants", heading: "35-39. Five Common Mistakes", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "dp[i][0] = 0", note: "Wrong -- should be 1. There is exactly one way to form an empty target: select nothing. Probably the most common bug in this problem." },
        { id: "2", title: "Using max(...) instead of +", note: "Wrong -- we're counting, not optimizing, so use addition." },
        { id: "3", title: "dp[i][j] = 0 on mismatch", note: "Wrong -- we can simply skip the source character: dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j]." },
        { id: "4", title: "Using dp[i][j-1] on mismatch", note: "Wrong -- the target is fixed; we only scan the source. We only ever skip source[i-1], never a target character independently." },
        { id: "5", title: "Forward loop in the 1D optimization", note: "Wrong -- may reuse the same source character more than once. Use j = target.length() down to 1." }
      ]},
      { type: "callout", heading: "40. Interview Explanation", tone: "gold",
        body: "'dp[i][j] represents the number of ways to form the first j characters of t using the first i characters of s. If the current characters don't match, the source character cannot be used, so we inherit dp[i-1][j]. If they match, we have two mutually exclusive choices: use it, contributing dp[i-1][j-1], or skip it, contributing dp[i-1][j]. Therefore we add those two counts.'" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "41. Pattern Classification", dark: true,
        body: "VARIATION: Sequence DP\n\nCore idea: count ordered selections.\nState: dp[i][j]\nTrigger: count ways to form target from source while preserving order.\n\nMatch: use + skip -> dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + dp[i-1][j]\nMismatch: skip source -> dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j]" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "42. Bigger Sequence DP Map", dark: true,
        body: "Sequence DP\n  |\n  +-- LCS\n  |     +-- SCS\n  |     +-- Delete Distance\n  |     +-- Uncrossed Lines\n  |\n  +-- Substring\n  |\n  +-- Edit Distance\n  |\n  +-- Distinct Subsequences\n        +-- COUNT ordered ways\n\nWhat matters isn't the number of problems -- it's the few underlying state/transition families." },
      { type: "practice", heading: "43. Your Pattern Recognition Checklist",
        intro: "When you get a string problem, ask:",
        items: [
          "1. One sequence or two?",
          "2. Contiguous? -> substring.",
          "3. Ordered but can skip? -> subsequence.",
          "4. Comparing two sequences? -> dp[i][j].",
          "5. Maximizing? -> max.",
          "6. Minimizing? -> min.",
          "7. Counting? -> +.",
          "8. Transforming? -> edit-distance family.",
          "9. Building a common sequence? -> LCS/SCS family."
        ],
        note: "This is the mental checklist that helps when the question is not a familiar LeetCode problem." },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 7 progress",
        items: [{ label: "68. LCS / Sequence DP", done: true }, { label: "69. Longest Common Substring", done: true },
          { label: "70. Edit Distance", done: true }, { label: "71. Shortest Common Superseq", done: true },
          { label: "72. Distinct Subsequences", done: true }, { label: "73. Interleaving String", done: false },
          { label: "74. LIS", done: false }, { label: "75. Palindrome DP", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Interleaving String",
        points: ["A particularly useful variation: the state is still dp[i][j], but i+j determines how much of a third string has been consumed", "Key pattern: two source strings, choose the next character from A or from B, both feeding the same target position", "Teaches a major DP concept: when one dimension is derivable from the others, you don't need to store it separately"] }
    ]
  },
  "interleaving-string": {
    title: "Pattern 73 -- Interleaving String",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "A Sequence DP variation that teaches a very important state-design idea: sometimes one piece of information can be derived from the other state variables, so we don't need to store it." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "1. The Problem", dark: true,
        body: "Given three strings s1, s2, s3, determine whether s3 can be formed by interleaving s1 and s2.\n\ns1=\"aabcc\", s2=\"dbbca\", s3=\"aadbbcbcac\" -> true, because we can preserve the internal order of both strings while mixing them.\n\nCharacters from s1 must remain in order, and characters from s2 must remain in order." },
      { type: "text", heading: "2. What Does Interleaving Mean?",
        body: "A=\"abc\", B=\"xyz\". Valid interleavings: \"abcxyz\", \"xaybzc\", \"axybzc\" -- provided a<b<c and x<y<z stay in relative order. Invalid: \"acbxyz\", because b and c from A are reversed." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "3. Recognition Triggers",
        intro: "Think Interleaving DP when you see:",
        items: ["combine two strings", "preserve order of both", "form a third string", "merge two sequences", "alternate/merge sequences", "choose next character from A or B", "determine whether a target can be formed from two sources"],
        note: "Strong trigger: can string C be formed by interleaving A and B while preserving the order within each?" },
      { type: "callout", heading: "4. The First Observation", tone: "red",
        body: "Before any DP, check lengths. If len(s1) + len(s2) != len(s3), it's immediately impossible -- return false. Every character of s1 and s2 must appear exactly once in the interleaving." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "5. The Mental Model", dark: true,
        body: "Imagine two queues: s1 -> a a b c c, s2 -> d b b c a. We're constructing s3. At every step: should I take the next character from s1 or from s2?\n\nThe DP state needs: how much of s1 have I consumed? how much of s2 have I consumed? -> dp[i][j]." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "6-7. State Definition & Why k = i + j", tone: "gold",
        body: "dp[i][j] = whether the first i characters of s1 and first j characters of s2 can form the first i+j characters of s3. Notice: s3 position = i + j.\n\nWe don't need dp[i][j][k], because k = i+j is always derivable -- every consumed character (from either source) goes straight into s3, so i+j characters of s3 have been consumed. This is the key state-compression insight." },
      { type: "text", heading: "8-10. Base Case, First Row, First Column",
        body: "dp[0][0] = true -- no characters from either string form an empty target.\n\nFirst row (j=0, using only s1): dp[i][0] = dp[i-1][0] && s1[i-1]==s3[i-1].\n\nFirst column (i=0, using only s2): dp[0][j] = dp[0][j-1] && s2[j-1]==s3[j-1]." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "11-14. The Main Transition", tone: "red",
        body: "At dp[i][j], the next character of s3 is s3[i+j-1]. Two possibilities:\n\nOption 1 -- take from s1: if s1[i-1]==s3[i+j-1], then dp[i][j] can come from dp[i-1][j].\nOption 2 -- take from s2: if s2[j-1]==s3[i+j-1], then dp[i][j] can come from dp[i][j-1].\n\nCombined: dp[i][j] = (s1[i-1]==s3[i+j-1] && dp[i-1][j]) OR (s2[j-1]==s3[i+j-1] && dp[i][j-1]).\n\nThe operator is OR, because we're asking: does at least one valid construction exist? This is boolean DP." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "15. Java Solution", dark: true,
        body: "public boolean isInterleave(String s1, String s2, String s3) {\n    int m = s1.length(), n = s2.length();\n    if (m + n != s3.length()) return false;\n\n    boolean[][] dp = new boolean[m + 1][n + 1];\n    dp[0][0] = true;\n\n    for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {\n        dp[i][0] = dp[i-1][0] && s1.charAt(i-1) == s3.charAt(i-1);\n    }\n    for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {\n        dp[0][j] = dp[0][j-1] && s2.charAt(j-1) == s3.charAt(j-1);\n    }\n\n    for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {\n        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {\n            int k = i + j - 1;\n            boolean fromS1 = dp[i-1][j] && s1.charAt(i-1) == s3.charAt(k);\n            boolean fromS2 = dp[i][j-1] && s2.charAt(j-1) == s3.charAt(k);\n            dp[i][j] = fromS1 || fromS2;\n        }\n    }\n\n    return dp[m][n];\n}\n\nComplexity: Time = O(mn), Space = O(mn)." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "16. The Most Important Mental Picture", dark: true,
        body: "             s2 ->\n          0  1  2  3\ns1   0\n v   1\n     2\n     3\n\nAt every cell: dp[i][j] can come from UP (take s1) or LEFT (take s2). The path through the grid represents the construction of s3." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "17. Why This Is Similar to LCS", items: [
        { label: "LCS", body: "dp[i][j] compares two sequences: what is the best common subsequence?" },
        { label: "Interleaving", body: "dp[i][j] tracks progress through two source sequences: can these two sources produce this target?" }
      ], conclusion: "Same dimensional structure, different semantics." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "18-19. Why Not dp[i][j][k]?", tone: "gold",
        body: "A beginner might define dp[i][j][k] where i=consumed from s1, j=consumed from s2, k=consumed from s3. But k = i+j always, so k adds no new information.\n\nFundamental DP optimization: don't store information that can be derived from other state variables. This generalizes: if state=(i,j) and some third quantity is always k=f(i,j), you probably don't need dp[i][j][k] -- this can reduce O(n^3) to O(n^2), a huge difference." },
      { type: "text", heading: "20-21. Worked Examples",
        body: "s1=\"ab\", s2=\"cd\", s3=\"acbd\": a<-s1, c<-s2, b<-s1, d<-s2 -- valid. Path: (0,0)->(1,0)->(1,1)->(2,1)->(2,2). Consumed 2 from s1, 2 from s2, 4 from s3.\n\ns1=\"aa\", s2=\"ab\", s3=\"aaba\": at some positions both strings offer 'a', so multiple valid paths can exist -- but we only need TRUE, so boolean DP is enough." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "22. Existence vs Counting", items: [
        { label: "Existence", body: "dp[i][j] = fromS1 || fromS2." },
        { label: "Number of ways", body: "dp[i][j] = waysFromS1 + waysFromS2." }
      ], conclusion: "Same structure, different objective -- if asked 'how many ways', switch boolean OR to addition." },
      { type: "variants", heading: "23-25. Common Mistakes", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Checking only s1+s2 or s2+s1", note: "Insufficient -- interleaving allows arbitrary mixing while preserving individual order, not just two concatenation orders." },
        { id: "2", title: "Checking only character frequencies", note: "s1=\"ab\", s3=\"bcad\" -- all characters present, but b appears before a, violating s1's required order. Order is the entire problem, not just presence." },
        { id: "3", title: "Greedily picking one source when both match", note: "If s1[i-1]==s3[k] AND s2[j-1]==s3[k], you must preserve both possibilities -- don't greedily choose one. That's exactly why DP (not a greedy pointer walk) is needed." }
      ]},
      { type: "callout", heading: "26. Why Greedy Fails", tone: "red",
        body: "If both sources offer the same next character, which one should you consume? A greedy choice may block a valid future construction. Branch + memoize is safer -- a classic example of 'local choice isn't necessarily globally optimal.'" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "27. Space Optimization", dark: true,
        body: "Each state depends only on the previous row plus the current row's left neighbor, so we can reduce to O(n) space:\n\nboolean[] dp = new boolean[n + 1];\ndp[0] = true;\nfor (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {\n    dp[j] = dp[j-1] && s2.charAt(j-1) == s3.charAt(j-1);\n}\n\nfor (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {\n    dp[0] = dp[0] && s1.charAt(i-1) == s3.charAt(i-1);\n    for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {\n        int k = i + j - 1;\n        boolean fromS1 = dp[j] && s1.charAt(i-1) == s3.charAt(k);\n        boolean fromS2 = dp[j-1] && s2.charAt(j-1) == s3.charAt(k);\n        dp[j] = fromS1 || fromS2;\n    }\n}\nreturn dp[n];\n\ndp[j] before the update represents the previous row." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "28-29. Recursive + Memoization", dark: true,
        body: "solve(i,j) = we've consumed i chars from s1 and j from s2. Next target position: k = i+j.\n\nif s1[i] matches s3[k]: try solve(i+1, j)\nif s2[j] matches s3[k]: try solve(i, j+1)\n\nMemoize (i,j) -- gives O(mn) states. Naive recursion branches exponentially, but many branches reach the same (i,j), so memo[(i,j)] collapses them. Repeated state -> DP." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "30. Pattern Classification", dark: true,
        body: "VARIATION: Sequence DP\n\nTrigger: two strings must form a third string while preserving order.\nState: dp[i][j]\nMeaning: can first i chars of s1 + first j chars of s2 form first i+j chars of s3?\n\nTransitions: take from s1 OR take from s2.\nKey optimization: k = i + j (derived, never stored)." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "31-32. Sequence DP Family Map & The Bigger Principle", dark: true,
        body: "Sequence DP\n  |\n  +-- LCS -> maximize\n  |     +-- Substring -> maximize contiguous run\n  |     +-- SCS -> minimize\n  |     +-- Distinct Subsequences -> count\n  |\n  +-- Edit Distance -> minimize\n  |\n  +-- Interleaving -> boolean existence\n\nThe bigger principle: same broad state shape -> different question -> different operator. MAX->LCS, MIN->Edit Distance, COUNT->Distinct Subsequences, BOOLEAN->Interleaving. Far more powerful than memorizing 100 recurrences." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "33. Interview Muscle Memory", tone: "gold",
        body: "When you see 'can I construct X from A and B while preserving the order of A and B?', ask: what have I consumed from A? what have I consumed from B? -> (i,j). Then: X position = i+j. Then: take A OR take B. That's the entire pattern." },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 7 progress",
        items: [{ label: "68. LCS / Sequence DP", done: true }, { label: "69. Longest Common Substring", done: true },
          { label: "70. Edit Distance", done: true }, { label: "71. Shortest Common Superseq", done: true },
          { label: "72. Distinct Subsequences", done: true }, { label: "73. Interleaving String", done: true },
          { label: "74. LIS", done: false }, { label: "75. Palindrome DP", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "LIS",
        points: ["A new major Sequence DP pattern with a different structure: one sequence + previous chosen element + increasing condition", "First the intuitive O(n^2) DP: dp[i] = LIS ending at i", "Then the important interview optimization: O(n^2) DP -> O(n log n) via Binary Search / Tails -- recognizing the optimization is half the problem"] }
    ]
  },
};
